WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.130
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.653
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:15.356
Unit two: Network Theory. Today you will describe structure using represented as / modeled as.

4
00:00:15.656 --> 00:00:18.331
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:22.267
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:23.897
Node.

7
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:28.636
Node means a point in a network, such as a person or computer.

8
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.865
Each airport is a node in the global network.

9
00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.114
Your turn. Say: Node.

10
00:00:37.967 --> 00:00:38.945
Node.

11
00:00:39.683 --> 00:00:40.545
Edge.

12
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.130
Edge means a connection between two nodes.

13
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:47.448
An edge may represent friendship or trade.

14
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.354
Your turn. Say: Edge.

15
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:53.992
Edge.

16
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.378
Network topology.

17
00:00:57.383 --> 00:01:00.973
Network topology means the pattern in which nodes are connected.

18
00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:05.104
Network topology determines how fast news spreads.

19
00:01:05.717 --> 00:01:09.112
Your turn. Say: Network topology.

20
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:13.560
Network topology.

21
00:01:14.317 --> 00:01:14.969
Hub.

22
00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:18.854
Hub means a node with many more connections than average.

23
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.030
Dubai is a hub for international flights.

24
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:24.654
Your turn. Say: Hub.

25
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.327
Hub.

26
00:01:29.083 --> 00:01:30.305
Centrality.

27
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:35.348
Centrality means a measure of how important a node is within a network.

28
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.215
Centrality identifies the most influential person.

29
00:01:39.817 --> 00:01:42.399
Your turn. Say: Centrality.

30
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:46.490
Centrality.

31
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.924
Small-world network.

32
00:01:49.917 --> 00:01:54.784
Small-world network means a network where any two nodes are connected by a few steps.

33
00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:59.031
A small-world network explains 'six degrees of separation'.

34
00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:02.699
Your turn. Say: Small-world network.

35
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:07.858
Small-world network.

36
00:02:08.617 --> 00:02:10.094
Scale-free network.

37
00:02:11.083 --> 00:02:15.788
Scale-free network means a network with a few huge hubs and many small nodes.

38
00:02:16.383 --> 00:02:19.486
The internet is often described as a scale-free network.

39
00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:23.397
Your turn. Say: Scale-free network.

40
00:02:26.233 --> 00:02:28.244
Scale-free network.

41
00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:30.054
Connectivity.

42
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.060
Connectivity means the degree to which nodes are linked.

43
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.578
Poor connectivity isolates rural communities.

44
00:02:38.183 --> 00:02:41.242
Your turn. Say: Connectivity.

45
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.368
Connectivity.

46
00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:49.539
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:50.383 --> 00:02:55.497
Visal, my professor says a village can be drawn as dots and lines. Isn't that too simple?

48
00:02:56.583 --> 00:03:05.064
A social system can be represented as a network. Each person is modelled as a node and each relationship as an edge.

49
00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.674
What do we learn from that?

50
00:03:08.783 --> 00:03:20.526
Centrality shows who matters most, and topology shows how quickly information travels. Hubs are often described as the system's key leverage points.

51
00:03:21.617 --> 00:03:22.862
Is it fragile, then?

52
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:31.940
A scale-free network is robust to random failure, yet it can be modelled as vulnerable to the loss of its hubs.

53
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.496
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.021
Grammar focus: Represented as and modeled as clauses.

55
00:03:39.321 --> 00:03:40.543
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.271
A social system can be represented as a network.

57
00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:47.206
Each person is modelled as a node.

58
00:03:48.117 --> 00:03:51.881
Hubs are often described as the system's key leverage points.

59
00:03:52.833 --> 00:04:03.160
Use passive + as: represented as, modelled as, described as, regarded as. 'As' introduces the category; do not add 'to be': modelled as a node (not 'as to be').

60
00:04:03.917 --> 00:04:08.450
Be careful! Don't say: A system can represent as a network.

61
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.688
Say: A system can be represented as a network.

62
00:04:13.988 --> 00:04:16.638
Passive: be + past participle.

63
00:04:17.433 --> 00:04:20.097
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.435
Seeing the Connections.

65
00:04:22.735 --> 00:04:26.883
Network theory studies relationships rather than isolated objects.

66
00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:34.266
A social system can be represented as a network, with people modelled as nodes and their relationships as edges.

67
00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:41.574
The topology, or pattern of connections, determines how quickly information, disease or money moves.

68
00:04:42.567 --> 00:04:50.905
Many real networks are scale-free: a few hubs hold most connections, so they are robust to random failure but vulnerable to targeted attack.

69
00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.931
Centrality measures therefore help policymakers decide where to intervene.

70
00:04:57.033 --> 00:05:00.942
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.783 --> 00:05:04.828
A social system can be represented blank a network.

72
00:05:05.128 --> 00:05:08.282
A, as. B, to. C, like to.

73
00:05:11.833 --> 00:05:13.682
The answer is A: as.

74
00:05:14.817 --> 00:05:17.362
A connection between two nodes is an blank.

75
00:05:17.662 --> 00:05:20.712
A, edge. B, hub. C, attractor.

76
00:05:24.267 --> 00:05:25.953
The answer is A: edge.

77
00:05:27.117 --> 00:05:30.242
A node with many more connections than average is a blank.

78
00:05:30.542 --> 00:05:33.801
A, hub. B, topology. C, edge.

79
00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:38.937
The answer is A: hub.

80
00:05:40.083 --> 00:05:41.769
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:47.964
A, People are modelled as nodes.. B, People are modelled to nodes.. C, People modelled as be nodes..

82
00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.498
The answer is A: People are modelled as nodes..

83
00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:59.020
According to the reading, scale-free networks are vulnerable to…

84
00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.844
A, random failure. B, targeted attack. C, no failure.

85
00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.852
The answer is B: targeted attack.

86
00:06:11.067 --> 00:06:14.508
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:18.673
What is an example of a network you interact with every day?

88
00:06:35.483 --> 00:06:37.018
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:37.318 --> 00:06:58.738
I interact with a social media network every day. Each user can be modelled as a node and each follow or friendship as an edge. A few accounts act as hubs with millions of followers, so information spreads through them very quickly. That structure explains why a single post can reach a whole country within hours.

90
00:06:59.038 --> 00:07:00.956
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:06.790
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and represented as and modeled as clauses.

92
00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:11.576
Today's value is analytical thinking. Look for the pattern underneath the surface.

93
00:07:11.876 --> 00:07:14.238
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:14.538 --> 00:07:16.993
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

